<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n50788" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">vexillārĭus</span></b> ,
		ii, m.
		vexillum. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>A standard-bearer</i>, <i>ensign.</i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>A.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>, <i><span class="en">Liv. 8, 8,
			4</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Tac. H. 1, 41</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>B.</b>
			<b>Transf.</b>, <i>a leader</i>, <i>captain</i> of a band of robbers,
					<i><span class="en">App. M. 4, p.
			146</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">vexillārĭi</span></b> ,
			ōrum, m., in the times of the
			emperors, <i>the oldest class of veterans</i>, <i>the last summoned</i>, <i><span class="en">Tac. A. 1,
			38</span></i>; <i><span class="en">id. H. 2, 83</span></i>; <i><span class="en">2, 100</span></i>;
					<i><span class="en">Veg. Mil. 2, 110 <i>fin.</i></span></i> al.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
